Call: a recipe line asks the running make and gets an answer. Every make process names
two descriptors to its recipes, AMK_CALL and AMK_REPLY, a request pipe and a reply pipe.
Everything is line framed and carries JSON as it is: a request is one line, the jq store's
operation, name and program, and a filter request says how many input lines follow it; a
reply is a status and count line, then that many output lines, one jq output each. The
parent answers while the recipe's shell runs: the blocking wait in reap_children and the
spawn wait both poll the request descriptors beside a pipe the SIGCHLD handler writes, so
a request never waits for the recipe to end and a death never waits for a poll interval.
A request that opens with a tag word is answered into a private FIFO under AMK_REPLY_DIR,
made and removed by the answering process and announced with one empty line on the reply
pipe, so callers that run at once never share a reader. A spawned job gets a pair of its
own, as it gets its mail pipe. A process that inherits a live pair, a sub-make or a job,
holds no store: its recipes write straight to the inherited pipe, and its own persistent
call travels the same way, so every process of a run reads and writes one set of entries.
A served request starts as an owner with fresh pipes, since the zygote closed every
inherited descriptor.

